[thought] Okay, the user wants me to list 25 distinct things that on-device AI can do that a cloud model can't. And each item needs two full sentences explaining it. Let me think through this.

First, I need to recall what on-device AI is. It's processing data locally on the phone, not sending it to the cloud. So, the things it can do would involve local computation, real-time processing, and maybe more privacy.

Starting with the basics. On-device AI can process data locally, which means it doesn't have to send data to the cloud. That's important for privacy. Also, real-time processing is a key point. For example, a phone can analyze video in real time, which a cloud model might not do as efficiently.

Then there's the aspect of low power consumption. On-device AI can use less power than cloud-based models, which is good for battery life. Also, the user might not have internet, so on-device AI is useful in that scenario.

Another point is model size. On-device models can be smaller, so they use less storage. Also, they can be more tailored to the user's environment, like a smartwatch with different sensors than a phone.

I should also think about specific applications.[/thought]
